xmodel/as109-quadratic-review-grok-20260824.md:100:The case directory contains only `FREEZE.sha256` and `verify_quadratic_no_go.py`. The replay is a finite regression control (formal support `{0,1,2,5}` plus four integer maps). The coefficient derivations below are the proofs for arbitrary `x`-degree.
xmodel/as109-quadratic-review-grok-20260824.md:102:A second sparse engine, written for this review and not importing the producer, checked: the `[y^3]`, mixed `[y^2]`, and affine `[y],[y^0]` identities on exponent sets including `{0,1,2,5}`, `{0,1,3,7,11}`, `{0,4,8,15}`, `{0,1,...,6,10,20}`, and `{0,108,109,216}` (0 failures); vanishing of `y^{>=4}` and of `y^{>=3}` after setting a leading quadratic to zero; the `a_1=0` expansion `J=a_0'(2 b_2 y+b_1)`; integer Keller, shear, `GL_2`, negative, and degenerate controls; a non-integral-`k` Wronskian; explicit triangular inverses in the both-nonzero, `a_1=0`, and `b_1=0` cases; thirty random AS109 shapes `P=x-x^{109}+109 A`, `Q=y+109 B` with `deg_y(A),deg_y(B)<=2` remaining of coordinate `y`-degree `<=2`; Fermat `a^{109}=a` on all of `F_{109}`; and `J(x-x^{109},y)=1-109 x^{108} \equiv 1 \pmod{109}`.
xmodel/as109-quadratic-review-grok-20260824.md:130:Characteristic zero gives `2 \neq 0`. The second engine reproduced (1.1) on every tested formal support, including degrees `108,109,216`, and found no `y^{>=4}` term.
xmodel/as109-quadratic-review-grok-20260824.md:163:(The factor `2` sits only on `a_1' b_2`, because `f_y=a_1` has no `y`.) The second engine reproduced (2.1) on every mixed formal support.
xmodel/as109-quadratic-review-grok-20260824.md:183:The constant `k` lives in `K`, not necessarily in a specified integral subring. Example over `Q`: `a_1=2x`, `b_2=x^2` gives `k=1/4` and vanishing `[y^2]`. That is why the shear is a field-level automorphy test and not an allowed `Z_{109}` support gauge.
xmodel/as109-quadratic-review-grok-20260824.md:222:Composing claims 1--3: start with `deg_y(f),deg_y(g)<=2` and `J(f,g)=j in K^*`. A constant target `GL_2(K)` makes one coordinate affine in `y`. A target shear of determinant one, with polynomial inverse, kills any remaining quadratic term. The resulting affine-in-`y` Keller pair has an explicit polynomial inverse over `K`. Automorphisms form a group, so the original pair is a polynomial automorphism of `A^2_K`.
xmodel/as109-quadratic-review-grok-20260824.md:242:A,B in Z_{109}[x,y],     deg_y(A), deg_y(B) <= 2,     det J(F)=1.
xmodel/as109-quadratic-review-grok-20260824.md:245:*Correction `y`-degree implies coordinate `y`-degree.* View `P,Q` in `Q_{109}[x,y]`. The summand `x-x^{109}` is independent of `y`, and `109\neq 0` in `Q_{109}`, so `deg_y(P)=deg_y(A)` if `deg_y(A)>=1`, and `deg_y(P)=0` otherwise. Likewise `deg_y(Q)=max(1, deg_y(B))` except possibly if the linear term of `109 B` cancelled the seed `y`. That cancellation would *drop* degree, so `deg_y(Q)<=2` still. (For completeness: it cannot occur for integral `B`, because the coefficient of `y` in `Q` is `1+109 b_1(x)` with `b_1 in Z_{109}[x]`, and `-1/109` is not in `Z_{109}`.) Thus `deg_y(P),deg_y(Q)<=2`. The identity `det J(F)=1` survives the inclusion `Z_{109}\to Q_{109}`. Claim 4 over `K=Q_{109}` makes `F` a polynomial automorphism of `A^2_{Q_{109}}`, hence injective on `Q_{109}^2`.
