xmodel/grok-vanrijn.md:105:**Object A — isotropic Schur recognition.** Expanding `det Hess(tP+Φ)` in `t` gives `[t^2]=0` and `[t]=-Φ_{mm} R(P)` with `R(P)=(∇P)^T adj(Hess P) ∇P`. Constant Hessian determinant forces `Φ_{mm}=0` or `R(P)=0`. The first branch *is* the cotangent/doubling chart; the second is a bordered-degeneracy branch already in his support-free reductions. The adjudication wrote the doubling as a *construction* (JC2 → a point of HC4). He writes the inverse recognition problem: every minimal 4-variable constant-Hessian collision should, after an allowed symplectic rechart, hit this flag, or the `R(P)=0` boundary must be classified until it cannot carry a collision. That problem is well-posed and was not in the Aug-21 spec. It is also unsolved in his notes.
