xmodel/secant-projective-review-grok-20260824.md:150:The projective closure of `V(I) ⊂ A^4 ⊂ P^4` is `Proj S/I^h = Proj S/(K:Z^∞)`. The naive scheme `Proj S/K` contains every component of that closure, and may contain extra components supported on `Z=0`, namely the `Z`-torsion of `S/K`. Dehomogenizing `K` at `Z=1` recovers an ideal with the same radical as `I` (in the examples below, recovers `I` exactly), so the extra components are invisible in the affine chart.
xmodel/secant-projective-review-grok-20260824.md:287:So the naive off scheme is supported on `Z=0`. Independent `primdecGTZ` returns exactly the two infinity primes `(Z,X-U)` and `(Z,X+U)`: adjoining `E` kills the diagonal and leaves only `Z`-torsion. Colon chain:
xmodel/secant-projective-review-grok-20260824.md:296:`sat_with_exp` returns exponent `3`, and `3` is **minimal**. Rabinowitsch Groebner basis of `(H_P,H_Q,E,1-t Z)` is `(1)`. Thus the honest off closure is empty, while the naive homogenization has only infinity-supported residue. This is an automorphism falsifier for the idea that a projective component of the homogenized equations is an off-diagonal sheet of `F`.
